"03_struct_accessors.mst — Dolphin's SMALLTALK protocol over two generated
 structs.

 IT NO LONGER SUPPLIES FIELD ACCESSORS. It used to: `genstructs` could not
 size a field whose winkb type is a Win32 ENUM (`MENUINFO_MASK`,
 `MENUITEMINFOW_STATE`), emitted a comment instead of an accessor, and
 `UI.Menu>>applyFlags` fell in the hole. The eight accessors that filled it
 were hand-checked against their neighbours and recorded in
 docs/LOOSE_ENDS.md with a named retirement: resolve an enum-typed field to
 its underlying integer type.

 THAT RETIREMENT HAS HAPPENED. winkb records `size_bits` for an enum just as
 it does for a struct — `LIST_VIEW_ITEM_STATE_FLAGS` is 32 — and
 `field_accessor` now follows it, so all eight are generated, at the same
 offsets the hand-written ones used. They are deleted rather than left as
 duplicates, because a second definition of a generated accessor is a silent
 override waiting for the generator to change.

 WHAT REMAINS is the layer that was never the generator's to emit: Dolphin's
 own Smalltalk over the layout, from `OS.MENUINFO.cls` and
 `OS.MENUITEMINFOW.cls`. It is transcribed rather than translated because
 those `.cls` files also carry a `defineFields` template, and emitting either
 as a class would REPLACE the generated layout with one that has no offsets at
 all — the same shape as the `.pax` incident that overwrote this very struct
 in DD10.

 (The `--reopen` path added for `OS.LVCOLUMNW` in DD11 is the general answer
 to that, and these two are its next candidates. Not converted in the same
 change that introduced it: both are on the menu path, which is green, and
 the DD11 gate needed the ListView.)

 IMAGES ARE NOT SUPPORTED and that is why the image branch is absent rather
 than stubbed: `setImage:metrics:` needs work beyond the graphics wave. A menu
 item with an image gets its text and id and no bitmap, which is a visible
 difference, not a silent one."
ExternalMemory subclass: MENUINFO [
    "Dolphin's own spellings. `Menu>>applyFlags` writes `struct style: flags`,
     and `Menu>>queryStyle` reads it back."
    style [ ^self dwStyle ]
    style: v [
        "MIM_STYLE = 16r10. Setting the style without ALSO setting the mask
         bit that says 'the style field is meaningful' makes SetMenuInfo
         ignore it — the call succeeds and nothing changes, which is the
         quietest possible failure. Dolphin own MENUINFO style: sets
         both; this does the same rather than leaving it to the caller."
        self fMask: (self fMask bitOr: 16r10).
        ^self dwStyle: v
    ]
]

"MENUITEMINFOW — the same enum-width gap, three fields, and the Smalltalk
 protocol Dolphin puts on top of the layout.

 Offsets pinned by the neighbours the generator DID emit:

     cbSize   uint32 at 1   (offset 0)
     fMask    ?      at 5   (offset 4)    <- supplied
     fType    ?      at 9   (offset 8)    <- supplied
     fState   ?      at 13  (offset 12)   <- supplied
     wID      uint32 at 17  (offset 16)

 `commandMenuItem:metrics:` and `basicText:` are Dolphin's own, from
 `OS.MENUITEMINFOW.cls`, transcribed rather than translated: that `.cls` also
 carries a `defineFields` template, and emitting it as a class would REPLACE
 the generated layout with one that has no offsets at all — the same shape as
 the `.pax` incident that overwrote this very struct in DD10.

 IMAGES ARE NOT SUPPORTED and that is why the image branch is absent rather
 than stubbed: `setImage:metrics:` needs the Graphics wave (DD11). A menu item
 with an image will get its text and id and no bitmap, which is a visible
 difference, not a silent one."
ExternalMemory subclass: MENUITEMINFOW [
    "Dolphin's `basicText:`. The buffer must OUTLIVE the SetMenuItemInfo call
     that reads through the pointer, which it does because the caller holds
     this struct until the send returns — the same lifetime rule the create
     path relies on for lpClassName."
    basicText: aString [
        | buf |
        buf := Utf16Buffer fromString: aString displayString.
        self dwTypeData: buf address.
        ^buf
    ]

    "MIIM_STRING = 16r40, MIIM_ID = 16r2, MIIM_FTYPE = 16r100, MIIM_SUBMENU
     = 16r4."
    "Dolphin's `type:` — set fType and mark ONLY that field meaningful.
     `DividerMenuItem>>populateItemInfo:metrics:` uses it: a separator has no
     text and no id, so the mask must not claim it has.
     MIIM_FTYPE = 16r100, assigned rather than OR-ed, exactly as Dolphin does."
    type: anInteger [
        self fType: anInteger.
        ^self fMask: 16r100
    ]

    commandMenuItem: aCommandMenuItem metrics: aSystemMetrics [
        | mask fType |
        mask := 16r40 bitOr: 16r2.
        fType := aCommandMenuItem styleFlags.
        fType = 0 ifFalse: [
            self fType: fType.
            mask := mask bitOr: 16r100 ].
        self basicText: aCommandMenuItem text.
        self wID: aCommandMenuItem id.
        self fMask: mask.
        ^self
    ]
]

"── TVINSERTSTRUCTW and TVITEMEXW — the tree-insert pair ────────────────
 THE GENERATOR CANNOT SIZE THIS ONE, and the reason is specific: Win32Metadata
 models `TVINSERTSTRUCTW`'s `item` as an ANONYMOUS UNION
 (`_Anonymous_e__Union`) recorded as a 64-bit primitive, so genstructs emits
 `sizeInBytes 24` for a structure that is really 96 — 8 for hParent, 8 for
 hInsertAfter, and an inline 80-byte TVITEMEXW at offset 16.

 That is not a missing accessor, it is an UNDER-ALLOCATION: `newBuffer` would
 hand comctl32 a 24-byte buffer to read 96 bytes out of. So the size is
 restated here, DERIVED from the two numbers the generator did get right —
 `_OffsetOf_Anonymous` (16) and `TVITEMEXW sizeInBytes` (80) — rather than
 written as a literal, so it follows the metadata if the layout ever changes.

 RETIREMENT: resolve an anonymous-union member to its largest alternative in
 `genstructs`. winkb records the union's members; the generator does not walk
 them. Recorded in docs/LOOSE_ENDS.md.

 The CONSTRUCTORS are Dolphin's own, from `OS.TVINSERTSTRUCTW.cls` and
 `OS.TVITEMW.cls`, transcribed rather than `--reopen`ed because both build on
 Dolphin's `CallbackPrototype` class constant and its `bytes:`/`shallowCopy`
 protocol — an ExternalStructure mechanism this port does not have. Written
 directly, the same values reach the same fields."
ExternalMemory subclass: TVITEMEXW [
    "Dolphin's `allCallbacks`: ask the control to CALL BACK for text, image
     and child count rather than storing them. This is what makes a virtual
     tree virtual — the item goes in with no data and TVN_GETDISPINFO fetches
     it on paint.

     I_IMAGECALLBACK and I_CHILDRENCALLBACK are -1; LPSTR_TEXTCALLBACK is the
     same bit pattern read unsigned, which is why it goes through `pszText:`
     (a pointer field) as -1 rather than 16rFFFFFFFF.
     TVIF_TEXT 1 | TVIF_IMAGE 2 | TVIF_CHILDREN 16r40 = 67."
    allCallbacks [
        self iImage: -1.
        self cChildren: 16rFFFFFFFF.
        self pszText: -1.
        ^self mask: (self mask bitOr: 67)
    ]

    TVITEMEXW class >> callbacksForItem: anExternalHandle [
        ^self newBuffer allCallbacks hItem: anExternalHandle; yourself
    ]
]

ExternalMemory subclass: TVINSERTSTRUCTW [
    TVINSERTSTRUCTW class >> sizeInBytes [
        ^TVINSERTSTRUCTW _OffsetOf_Anonymous + TVITEMEXW sizeInBytes
    ]
    TVINSERTSTRUCTW class >> byteSize [ ^self sizeInBytes ]
    byteSize [ ^TVINSERTSTRUCTW sizeInBytes ]
    TVINSERTSTRUCTW class >> new [ ^self new: TVINSERTSTRUCTW sizeInBytes ]

    "The inline TVITEMEXW, as a TYPED view so its own accessors are reachable.
     A generic view would answer raw bytes and `item allCallbacks` would be a
     doesNotUnderstand."
    item [
        ^TVITEMEXW viewOn: self
            at: TVINSERTSTRUCTW _OffsetOf_Anonymous
            size: TVITEMEXW sizeInBytes
    ]

    "Dolphin's `hParent:hInsertAfter:`. The item is set to all-callbacks here,
     as Dolphin's `initialize` does when it builds its CallbackPrototype —
     every caller of this constructor is a VIRTUAL update mode, which adds
     items with no data and answers the callbacks later."
    TVINSERTSTRUCTW class >> hParent: parentHandle hInsertAfter: siblingHandle [
        | s |
        s := self newBuffer.
        s hParent: parentHandle.
        s hInsertAfter: siblingHandle.
        s item allCallbacks.
        ^s
    ]
]
